---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What's the best way to digitize old documents?",
    a: "Inventory first, digitize the most-requested records first, handle fragile items as little as possible, capture with whatever equipment fits the material (sheet-fed scanner, flatbed, overhead, or phone), name files consistently from day one, run OCR, and keep at least three copies of the results.",
  },
  {
    q: "What resolution should I scan historical documents at?",
    a: "300 dpi is a common minimum for text documents used as access copies. Preservation-grade masters follow imaging standards such as FADGI and usually call for specialist equipment.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I digitize documents without a scanner?",
    a: "Yes. A modern phone produces readable, OCR-able images of most pages if you control light, angle and focus. It's also the gentlest option for bound or fragile items, which shouldn't go through a sheet-fed scanner.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I throw away the originals after digitizing?",
    a: "Not historical records. Digital copies are for access; the originals remain the record. Only destroy material your retention policy says can go, and only after confirming the digital copies are complete.",
  },
];
---
<ContentLayout
  title="Digitizing Historical Documents: A 7-Step Guide for Small Archives | Boxchive"
  description="How to digitize old and fragile documents: inventory, prioritize, handle safely, capture with a scanner or phone, name files, make text searchable, and back everything up."
  path="/guides/digitizing-historical-documents/"
  h1="Digitizing historical documents: a 7-step guide"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  lede="Digitizing old records goes well when you decide the boring things up front — what you have, what matters most, how files will be named — and badly when you start scanning box one and hope to sort it out later. Here's the order that works for small archives."
  crumbs={[{ name: "Guides", path: "/guides/" }]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={["/archive-digitization/", "/guides/file-naming-conventions/", "/guides/handwriting-ocr/"]}
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Inventory</strong> what you have, roughly.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize</strong> by what people actually use.</li>
    <li><strong>Handle safely</strong> — less handling is better.</li>
    <li><strong>Capture</strong> with equipment that fits the material.</li>
    <li><strong>Name files</strong> consistently from the first page.</li>
    <li><strong>Make the text searchable</strong> with OCR and basic details.</li>
    <li><strong>Store and back up</strong> in at least three places.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>1. Inventory what you have</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need a finding aid to start, just a list. For each box or series, note what's in it,
    the rough date range, condition, format (loose sheets, bound volumes, clippings, oversized),
    and whether it's typed or handwritten. A spreadsheet is fine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The inventory tells you how big the job is, which equipment you need, and where the hard pages
    are. It's also exactly what any vendor — or Boxchive — needs to give you an honest estimate.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Prioritize by use, not shelf order</h2>
  <p>Start with records that get asked for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Founding documents, charters, bylaws, deeds.</li>
    <li>Board and committee minutes.</li>
    <li>Registers and membership rolls.</li>
    <li>Whatever supports an upcoming anniversary, exhibit, history, or grant application.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Then add anything visibly deteriorating, so at least an access copy exists.</p>

  <h2>3. Handle safely</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Work on a clean, dry, flat surface away from food and drink.</li>
    <li>Many conservators, including the Library of Congress, advise clean, dry hands rather than
      cotton gloves for most paper, because gloves reduce dexterity and make tears more likely.</li>
    <li>Don't force bound volumes flat; support the spine and photograph pages at the angle the
      binding allows.</li>
    <li>Don't remove rusted staples or old tape yourself if the paper is brittle — leave it and note
      it.</li>
    <li>Fragile and brittle items should never go through a sheet-fed scanner.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Capture: scanner, overhead, or phone</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Good for</th><th>Avoid for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sheet-fed scanner</td><td>Sturdy, loose, standard-size paper</td><td>Anything fragile, bound, or stapled</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flatbed scanner</td><td>Single fragile sheets, photos, clippings</td><td>Large volumes of pages (slow); thick bound books</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overhead / book scanner</td><td>Bound volumes, high volume, preservation work</td><td>Small budgets — specialist equipment</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Phone camera</td><td>Bound and fragile items in place; mixed material; no equipment budget</td><td>Preservation-grade masters; large-format items</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    For text documents meant as access copies, 300 dpi is a common minimum. For phone photos, what
    matters is even light without glare, the camera held parallel to the page, the whole page in
    frame, and sharp focus. Preservation masters made to standards such as FADGI need specialist
    equipment.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Name files from the first page</h2>
  <p>
    Renaming thousands of files later is miserable. Pick a convention — ideally the one your paper
    files already use — and apply it from day one. A pattern like
    <code>1962-03-14_board-minutes_p01</code> sorts by date and tells you what a file is without
    opening it. Our <a href="/guides/file-naming-conventions/">file naming guide</a> has rules,
    examples and a builder.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Make the text searchable</h2>
  <p>
    An image isn't searchable until OCR turns it into text. Add basic details — date, document
    type, key names — and the archive answers real questions like "all minutes from the 1970s" or
    "every letter mentioning the building fund." See
    <a href="/searchable-archive/">what makes an archive searchable</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Handwritten pages are the hard case: standard OCR does little with old cursive. Read
    <a href="/guides/handwriting-ocr/">handwriting OCR for old records</a> before promising anyone
    that the registers will be searchable.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Store and back up</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the 3-2-1 rule: keep three copies, on two different kinds of storage, with one copy
    off-site. Check the copies periodically — a backup nobody has opened in five years isn't one.
    Keep the originals; digital copies are for access, and the paper is still the record. Use your
    <a href="/guides/nonprofit-document-retention-policy/">retention policy</a> to decide what, if
    anything, can be destroyed.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Starting capture before deciding on filenames.</li>
    <li>Scanning to images with no OCR, then wondering why nothing is searchable.</li>
    <li>Treating handwritten pages as done when nothing could read them.</li>
    <li>Keeping the only copy on one laptop or one USB drive.</li>
    <li>Trying to digitize everything before anyone can use anything — finish the high-value series
      first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Doing it with Boxchive</h2>
  <p>
    Boxchive covers steps 4 through 6 with a phone in place of a scanner: your staff or volunteers
    photograph the pages, and Boxchive runs OCR, extracts dates and names, classifies each page by
    document type, files it under your naming convention, and gives you a keyword-searchable
    archive — flagging handwritten and fragile pages for human review.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
